 - **[Plugin Scope] The plugin loads only into wrapped sessions via `--plugin-dir`.** Both driver and driven run under `viola run`. `viola plugin install` (a local marketplace) is optional, for the MCP verbs in an unwrapped driver. The installed plugin carries only `.mcp.json` and no hooks, so a wrapped session that also has it installed never runs two hook processes for one event (two `hook.dialog` requests for one dialog). Its `command` is the absolute path of the exe that ran `plugin install`, never a PATH lookup. Whether Claude Code loads both a `--plugin-dir` plugin and an installed plugin with the same name, and which `viola` MCP server a wrapped session then uses, is a capability-ledger row. Rationale: unwrapped sessions carry no viola hooks at all, so there is no per-tool-call spawn cost outside viola. The exceptions are `viola verify`'s transient unwrapped children, all gone when verify ends. The first is the print-mode probe, an unwrapped `claude -p` that loads only the `viola-verify-probe` plugin (four exec-form `hook <event> --capture` hooks) from `ledger/probes/<pid>/plugin/`. The other five are the interactive runs: Run A, untrusted in an OS-temp `viola-verify-*` dir and killed at its screen; Run B, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>/`; Runs C and D, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-dialogs/` and `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-plan/`, each loading the dialog-kind `viola-verify-probe` plugin; and Run E, trusted in `<cwd>/.viola-verify-<pid>-resume/`, loading the capture plugin from `ledger/probes/<pid>/resume/plugin/` and resuming Run B's first session with `--resume <id>`, no key typed. Runs B, C and D, as real interactive turns, also run the user's own global hooks, and Runs C and D, and Run B on Windows, the user's own status line (on Unix Run B's status line is the probe capture its one probe `--settings` names, [CLI Version Compatibility]), and each leaves the CLI's session transcripts under `~/.claude/projects/` (an accepted residual, security